---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { appUrl, billingOpen, breadcrumbs } from "../lib/seo";
const open = billingOpen();
---

<Base schema={[breadcrumbs([["Pricing", "/pricing"]])]}>
  <h1>Pricing</h1>
  <p class="lead">Messages are counted, not billed. The carrier plan on your SIM is what actually sends them, so what you pay simhook for is the routing, the receipts, the webhooks, and the dashboard.</p>

  <section>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Plan</th><th>Messages</th><th>Phones</th><th>Per send</th><th>Price</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Free</td><td>30 a day, 500 a month</td><td>1</td><td>25 recipients</td><td class="nowrap">$0</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Pro</td><td>10,000 a month</td><td>3</td><td>500 recipients</td><td class="nowrap">$12 / month, or $120 / year</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Scale</td><td>50,000 a month</td><td>10</td><td>2,500 recipients</td><td class="nowrap">$39 / month, or $390 / year</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    {open ? (
      <p class="cap">Upgrade under <a href={`${appUrl()}/billing`}>Billing</a> in the dashboard. Nothing you build changes when you upgrade.</p>
    ) : (
      <p class="cap">Paid plans open soon. Every account is on Free until then, and nothing you build changes when you upgrade.</p>
    )}
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>what counts</h2>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><b>One message</b><span>One recipient. A send to twenty numbers is twenty messages.</span></li>
      <li><b>Long texts</b><span>A text the phone splits into several SMS still counts once.</span></li>
      <li><b>Received texts</b><span>Free and uncounted on every plan.</span></li>
      <li><b>Failed sends</b><span>Count once the server accepts them, whether or not the phone manages to send. Scheduled sends count on the day they go out.</span></li>
      <li><b>Days and months</b><span>Calendar days and months in UTC.</span></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>every plan has</h2>
    <p>The full API, signed webhooks with retries, delivery receipts, dual-SIM support, scheduling, the dashboard, the TypeScript SDK, the MCP server, and the app with its updates. Limits are the only difference.</p>
  </section>

  {open ? (
    <section>
      <h2>billing</h2>
      <ul class="plain">
        <li><b>Who charges you</b><span>Polar Software, Inc., our merchant of record, sells the plans, issues the invoice, and handles tax. Its name is on the card statement. simhook never sees your card.</span></li>
        <li><b>Tax</b><span>Prices are in US dollars before tax. VAT or sales tax is added at checkout where it applies, from your billing address.</span></li>
        <li><b>Renewal</b><span>A plan renews monthly or yearly until you cancel it in the dashboard. A cancelled plan runs to the end of the paid period, then the account is on Free.</span></li>
        <li><b>Changing plans</b><span>Moving up is charged and applied right away, less what the period already paid for. Moving down applies at the next renewal.</span></li>
        <li><b>Refunds</b><span>A charge you did not mean to make is refunded if you write to <a href="mailto:hello@simhook.dev">hello@simhook.dev</a> within 14 days of it.</span></li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  ) : null}

  <section>
    <h2>self-hosting</h2>
    <p>The whole stack is open source under the AGPL-3.0, and running it yourself is free with no limits of any kind. <a href="/docs/self-hosting">How to self-host</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
